    {
      "id": "0a6852a7-9595-4e61-98e6-a016940508aa",
      "body": "Mechanically yes \u2014 but it's not plumbed in today: the classification head takes only the pooled Surya embedding, so you'd need to modify it to concatenate normalized lat/lon onto that embedding before the final linear layer, similar in spirit to how the backbone already gets time metadata, but applied at the head instead.\n\nThe bigger issue is scientific, not architectural: filament chirality is known to follow a hemispheric pattern (Martin's rule) \u2014 northern-hemisphere filaments are predominantly dextral, southern predominantly sinistral, correct ~90% of the time just from sign of latitude alone. So handing the model latitude risks letting it (or your \"baseline\") solve the task via that shortcut rather than the actual image content, which would undercut the comparison you're trying to make between Surya and a naive baseline.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789499588.2992048
    },
    {
      "body": "That's fine, that's the exact toy model I want to solve. I will give Surya the hemisphere or Latitude/Longitude and I want to see if it can perform even close to the known pattern. I know it's not a sophisticated scientific outcome, but I want something simple to test my development on Surya architecture.",
      "metadata": {
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "model": {
          "id": "sonnet",
          "settings": {}
        },
        "settings": {}
      },
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789499702.712484,
      "sender": "kwebster",
      "id": "b06e313a-4adc-4abc-a0f2-d20cea7adcdb",
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "0376ce2c-f153-4527-b8c2-5146c36faabf",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "That's a solid rationale \u2014 using a known, strong pattern as ground truth means you have a clear signal to sanity-check the whole pipeline (data loading, head architecture, training loop) against, rather than hoping a weak/noisy signal shows up in 45 samples. The \"shortcut\" concern I raised isn't a problem here since it's literally the thing you're testing for.\n\nThis will touch a few pieces: adding lat/lon (or hemisphere) columns to your catalog, updating `FilamentDataset` to carry them through, and modifying the classification head to concatenate them with the pooled embedding. Want me to put together an implementation plan for that?",
      "time": 1789499718.0766292
    }
  ],
